                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 P?S Extended PDP-8 Simulator (PEPS) Package STRIP.TEC Macro Internal Description \par
\par
Copyright \'a9 1975-1989, Charles Lasner Associates\par
Copyright \'a9 1990-2018, \cf0\'bbCLA Systems\cf1\par
ClaSystems@gmail.com\par
\par
Last edit: 21-Aug-2018 cjl\par
\par
Technical level: Intermediate+.  Some working knowledge of TECO required; access to\par
a TECOC reference document is highly recommended to fully understand some of the\par
more arcane TECO commands used.  (Note: Advanced TECO users may use a larger subset\par
of TECOC in their manual editing work; however, some of the commands documented here\par
will exceed even their working knowledge of TECO as well; certain commands are\par
hardly ever used outside of TECO macros; some are virtually exclusive to TECOC.)\par
\par
Introduction.\par
\par
Various components of the P?S Extended PDP-8 Simulator (PEPS) package (an extension\par
to the SIMH PDP-8 simulator) for Windows use the STRIP.TEC macro file to filter and\par
correct various file quirks in output files created within the SIMH simulated PDP-8\par
environment.  These aberrations are usually caused by minor programming errors that\par
went virtually unnoticed when originally used on then-present day hardware.  This is\par
because physical lineprinters and similar output devices generally could tolerate\par
modest formatting errors (and many programmers were complacent).\par
\par
Prior to the use of simulators, printer users were willing to tear off output\par
perhaps after manually separating pages (or at least removing extraneous pages on\par
either side of the listing).  Few programmers cared about minor anomalies; merely\par
obtaining a listing was often a luxury.\par
\par
Note: Some users were especially picky regarding the way fan-folded listings were folded, preferring a top page with the fold at the bottom; as necessary, this could include an additional blank page after the last printed page with the fold at the top.  Failing to obtain this precise result led to manually refolding the pages. Repeat printing the listing merely to obtain the desired result was considered prohibitive.  Savvy users learned the behavior of specific lineprinters to generally\par
assure the final result would be obtained without resorting to refolding; however, if the only "problem" was the "wrong" fold direction, listings were generally manually refolded while waiting for the next file to print; access to lineprinters\par
was typically so limited that elaborate scenarios were planned to obtain printed\par
output sometimes from relatively remote computer installations.\par
\par
Processing output files.\par
\par
Today we expect to either read the lineprinter-oriented output directly in an editor\par
such as Windows Notepad, or perhaps publish the output through a word-processor or a\par
.PDF file creator.  As such, all minor aberrations must first be removed for the\par
benefit of these "modern" facilities, which tend to easily reveal such quirks (which\par
are often dealt with in a heavy-handed manner).  (Generally, fan-fold paper is no\par
longer used, but may be simulated using available utilities.)\par
\par
The burden of printing has shifted to post-processing within the host environment\par
(in this instance Windows or perhaps MS-DOS) which can be somewhat "hostile" to the\par
raw output created by prevailing programs such as OS/8 PAL8 and P?S/8 PAL.  In\par
certain cases, the output can be tainted by the deliberate insertion of\par
inappropriate characters as part of an ill-conceived design (which was demanded by\par
programming managers with a twisted agenda).  The reasoning behind all of the\par
problems dealt with here is beyond the scope of this document; the only concern is\par
how STRIP.TEC resolves them.\par
\par
The PEPS Package enhancements allow easy review of the simulated paper-tape and\par
lineprinter output files.  For those interested in viewing specific quirks, the\par
files can be displayed before and after STRIP.TEC is applied.\page Note: Viewing OS/8-generated text files before filtering may prove difficult; the files are usually created with mark parity set on every character.  Editors are available such as EditPad Lite, which can interpret text encoding as seven-bit ASCII\par
using nonstandard settings.\par
\par
TECO macro arguments and execution.\par
\par
The TECO macro STRIP.TEC is launched by the following command inside several batch\par
files which are initiated by the shortcuts (Commands) section of the package:\par
\par
TECOC MUNG STRIP.TEC,%STRIPFILE%\par
\par
The STRIPFILE environment variable contents are derived from the %1 argument as passed to the relevant batch files.  By specifying an independent variable, flexibility in command formation is achieved and readability is enhanced.  Generally the contents of STRIPFILE will be the complete pathname of the file to be processed.\par
\par
The MUNG sub-command places the entire contents of STRIP.TEC into an internal buffer\par
within TECOC.\par
\par
Note: Various implementations of TECO have equivalent startup mechanisms which may not be compatible with TECOC.  The differences, although generally minor, must be dealt with as required.  STRIP.TEC is designed to work with only TECOC; minor changes may be necessary to use STRIP.TEC with other TECO implementations.\par
\par
As used here, the contents of the STRIPFILE environment variable are placed into the edit buffer; STRIP.TEC execution commences as the contents of an internal buffer\par
(equivalent to an internal unnamed Q-register).\par
\par
Note: Startup conventions and exact mechanisms used to pass command-line arguments to TECO are extremely implementation-specific.  In some versions, the string is passed as the text within a Q-register perhaps different from another Q-register within which are the macro contents; see specific TECO release documentation for more information regarding startup and initial argument passing considerations.\par
 \par
Except where otherwise indicated, $ refers to the escape character (<ESC>) (ASCII code 27 decimal, 32 octal), which has no defined printing character; when viewing a macro within most versions of TECO, "$" characters are displayed as a substitution for the escape character; this includes displaying escape characters within the edit buffer using any of the usual viewing commands.  This is why it is difficult to edit\par
TECO macros other than within TECO itself.  The escape character is used as a terminator of a string or command in most instances; a macro must contain escape characters by nature.  The situation is often complicated as TECO macros are known to dynamically create other TECO macros to be executed from the TECO macro program.  As such, these created TECO macros will also contain escape characters.\par
\par
The \'a7 character is used to show a continuation of a long line within this document.\par
(The actual line contents does not include the \'a7 character.)\par
\par
Fortunately, TECO allows direct insertion of individual characters by character\par
code.  The sequence 27I$ is often used for the purpose of inserting <ESC>\par
characters.\par
\par
Note: Some programmers are more comfortable with octal values; by executing the Control-O command, all further numeric input will be evaluated in octal radix until a Control-D command is executed to return the input radix to decimal.  In that context, the sequence 32I$ is often used to achieve the equivalent result.  Future releases of STRIP.TEC may use octal radix notation given the number of special case character codes processed.)\page Similarity of different commands.\par
\par
The Insert (I) command, when passed a numerical argument (and terminated by an\par
escape character), creates a single character in the edit buffer with the code of\par
the numerical argument.  The numerical form of the "I" command requires a trailing\par
escape character to distinguish this from the text form of the "I" command which\par
(potentially) allows multiple insertions of text strings.\par
\par
Note: Specifics of the text form of the "I" command could be version-dependent. STRIP.TEC avoids command extensions where possible.  The text form of the "I" command (as typically used when editing files manually) inserts the entirety of an indefinite text string into the edit buffer when the string terminator is reached. However, it might be used to insert one text character (as opposed to a numerically evaluated character).\par
\par
Various TECO commands have an analogous duality of functionality determined by exact\par
usage.  In some instances, the contents of TECO macros must be carefully studied to\par
determine the exact form of the command being used.\par
\par
The description of the entire STRIP.TEC file follows.  Further below, each section\par
is explained in detail as to what its purpose is.  Please note that line breaks\par
(<CR>/<LF>) are meaningless within TECO except when those characters would be part\par
of a string passed to the textual form of the "I" command.  Thus, line breaks were added for readability.\par
\par
Known Limitations.\par
\par
Due to the way OS/8 creates quirky output with the PTP: handler, it is possible for\par
trailing <FF> characters to occasionally still be present in the file after\par
stripping.  If necessary, the file may be stripped again to remove the trailing <FF>\par
character.  Since the last revision of STRIP.TEC, this problem has not been observed.\par
