EZ#128EZ$\tab\tab    EZ is a version-dependent internal register of TECOC which\par
\tab\tab\tab    contains various control functions which can affect overall\par
\tab\tab\tab    operation.  The value already contained in the EZ register\par
\tab\tab\tab    is inclusively .OR.ed with the constant 128 then written\par
\tab\tab\tab    back to the EZ register.  By forcing this specific bit to\par
\tab\tab\tab    the on state, buffer reads limited by <FF> characters are\par
\tab\tab\tab    inhibited.  Other TECO implementations could handle this\par
\tab\tab\tab    situation differently or not at all; the logic of the\par
\tab\tab\tab    STRIP.TEC macro depends on this operational behavior to\par
\tab\tab\tab    search for <FF> characters.\par
\par
HXQ\tab\tab\tab    All of the (wHole (H)) text in the edit buffer is copied (X)\par
\tab\tab\tab    into Q-register Q.  The referenced text is the passed\par
\tab\tab\tab    complete pathname argument, which is the expansion of the\par
\tab\tab\tab    PTO environment variable such as:\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    %PDP-8_DRIVE%\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\\SIMH_Working_Files\par
\tab\tab\tab    \'a7\\PTPFILE.TXT\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    which in turn contains the environment variable PDP-8_DRIVE.\par
\tab\tab\tab    This reduces to something of the form:\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    J:\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\\SIMH_Working_Files\\PTPFILE.TXT\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    This string is placed in the edit buffer by invoking TECOC in\par
\tab\tab\tab    this manner.  (Note: The PTO environment variable is generally\par
\tab\tab\tab    derived from the %1 argument to the batch file initiating\par
\tab\tab\tab    the TECOC command.  Command-line arguments to invoke TECO\par
\tab\tab\tab    are extremely system-dependent; this method is valid for the\par
\tab\tab\tab    MS-DOS and WINDOWS versions of TECOC.)\par
\par
HK\tab\tab\tab    Kills the entire (wHole (H)) passed edit buffer, since it is\par
\tab\tab\tab    now saved in Q-register Q.\par
\par
GQ\tab\tab\tab    Brings the passed string back into the edit buffer.  (Note:\par
\tab\tab\tab    This method is used to allow additional file commands\par
\tab\tab\tab    perhaps needed in a future version of the program.)\par
\par
0JIEB$\tab\tab    Jump to the beginning of the buffer and insert "EB" there.\par
\tab\tab\tab    The Edit Backup (EB) command, when fully formed, will be\par
\tab\tab\tab    executed as a TECO macro.\par
 \par
ZJ27I$\tab\tab    A trailing escape character is required to complete the\par
\tab\tab\tab    macro command when it is executed.  The buffer pointer is\par
\tab\tab\tab    set to point to the end of the edit buffer then an escape\par
\tab\tab\tab    character is placed into the edit buffer to complete the\par
\tab\tab\tab    command.\par
\par
HXB\tab\tab\tab    The entire (wHole (H)) edit buffer contents, which is\par
\tab\tab\tab    something of the form:\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    EBJ:\\PDP-8\\SIMH\\PDP-8\\SIMH_Working_Files\\PTPFILE.TXT$\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    is placed (X) into Q-register B.\par
\par
HK\tab\tab\tab    Kills the entire (wHole (H)) passed edit buffer, since it is\par
\tab\tab\tab    now saved in Q-register B.\par
\par
MB\tab\tab\tab    Q-register B's string contents is executed using a Macro B\par
\tab\tab\tab    (MB) command.  This sets up the passed file for editing.\page Line #2:\tab\tab    Y0J<N^X$;-1A&127U1-D\par
\par
Y\tab\tab\tab    The Yank (Y) command reads in some portion of the file into\par
\tab\tab\tab    the edit buffer.  Since <FF> checking is inhibited, only\par
\tab\tab\tab    overall buffer size determines the exact quantity read in at\par
\tab\tab\tab    any point.  TECO implementations vary as to exact edit\par
\tab\tab\tab    buffer size; a number of additional locations are reserved\par
\tab\tab\tab    in case the edit buffer grows larger.  Typical usage of the\par
\tab\tab\tab    STRIP.TEC program will generally maintain the overall file\par
\tab\tab\tab    size; certain specific operations might tend to raise or\par
\tab\tab\tab    lower the overall size slightly depending on specific file\par
\tab\tab\tab    contents.  Most character processing consists of merely\par
\tab\tab\tab    clearing the high-order (mark parity) bit.  As such, the\par
\tab\tab\tab    overall character count tends to remain constant.\par
\par
0J\tab\tab\tab    Reset the buffer pointer (J) to the beginning of the edit\par
\tab\tab\tab    buffer (0).  Most TECO implementations will do this\par
\tab\tab\tab    automatically after a Yank operation.  TECO programmers\par
\tab\tab\tab    often include this to clarify the scope of an iteration loop\par
\tab\tab\tab    that follows.\par
\par
<\tab\tab\tab    Because of the leading "<" character, what follows up to a\par
\tab\tab\tab    matching ">" character is executed in an infinite loop;\par
\tab\tab\tab    various methods exist to break out of the loop, such as a\par
\tab\tab\tab    failing search operation.  This is the primary operational\par
\tab\tab\tab    loop for most of the program.\par
\par
N^X$;\tab\tab\tab    A Nonstop search is performed; all characters processed will\par
\tab\tab\tab    be forced into the output buffer and (eventually) written\par
\tab\tab\tab    out.  In this case, the search is a special-case operation:\par
\tab\tab\tab    the single character Control-X.  This means the search\par
\tab\tab\tab    argument is any single character of any value.  The only way\par
\tab\tab\tab    the search can fail returns a special value that can be\par
\tab\tab\tab    detected by alternate means.  (This situation is not\par
\tab\tab\tab    searchable in the ordinary sense; it is a status of the\par
\tab\tab\tab    special condition of the buffer pointer position, not an\par
\tab\tab\tab    actual character.)\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    When any form of search inside of iteration brackets fails\par
\tab\tab\tab    and a trailing ";" character exists, the normal error abort\par
\tab\tab\tab    is inhibited; the iteration loop ends and execution\par
\tab\tab\tab    continues past the ">" character matching the "<" character\par
\tab\tab\tab    in effect when the failing search was attempted.  (Note: In\par
\tab\tab\tab    STRIP.TEC there is only one iteration loop; TECO allows\par
\tab\tab\tab    nesting of iteration loops.)\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Assuming the search succeeded, the buffer pointer is now\par
\tab\tab\tab    just past the matching character.  Should a character be\par
\tab\tab\tab    inserted, the buffer pointer will be placed just past the\par
\tab\tab\tab    newly inserted character.  In some instances the matching\par
\tab\tab\tab    character will be deleted (causing the buffer pointer to be\par
\tab\tab\tab    placed just past the previous character, if any).  (Note:\par
\tab\tab\tab    Character insertion or deletion after a match is only\par
\tab\tab\tab    performed in certain specific cases.)\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Control-X as used here is the single character Control-X\par
\tab\tab\tab    (ASCII code 24 decimal, 30 octal); in other contexts, the\par
\tab\tab\tab    two-character sequence "^" and "X" (or "x") is also allowed.\par
\tab\tab\tab    However, all search arguments in TECO must be stated\par
\tab\tab\tab    literally.  (Note: Most TECO implementations echo the\par
\tab\tab\tab    Control-X character as "^X"; when viewing macros such as\par
\tab\tab\tab    STRIP.TEC, the correct usage must be confirmed as display\par
\tab\tab\tab    operations are generally anomalous on this issue.)\page -1A&127U1-D\tab\tab    When the "A" command is used with a leading numerical\par
\tab\tab\tab    argument, the enumerated value is used as an offset from the\par
\tab\tab\tab    buffer pointer to locate a particular character in the\par
\tab\tab\tab    buffer.  In this instance the numeric value of the previous\par
\tab\tab\tab    character (-1) is returned (which is the character that\par
\tab\tab\tab    matched the search as described above).  This value is then\par
\tab\tab\tab    masked against the constant 127 (177 octal) via a logical\par
\tab\tab\tab    .AND. operation which preserves the low-order seven bits\par
\tab\tab\tab    while removing the high-order bit.  The filtered value is\par
\tab\tab\tab    then stored (U) in numeric Q-register 1 for further testing;\par
\tab\tab\tab    the original character (located one position before (-) the\par
\tab\tab\tab    current buffer pointer value) is then Deleted (D) from the\par
\tab\tab\tab    edit buffer.  (Later in the program, the test character will\par
\tab\tab\tab    generally be inserted into the edit buffer unless special\par
\tab\tab\tab    handling is required.)\par
