Q1-26"NONOTEND$'\tab    If the test character is not Control-Z (ASCII code 26\par
\tab\tab\tab    decimal, 32 octal), transfer control (using the gOtO (O)\par
\tab\tab\tab    command) to the portion of the program labeled \i !NOTEND!\i0 .  If\par
\tab\tab\tab    the character is Control-Z, execution continues inline to\par
\tab\tab\tab    finalize the file in the next code section.\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab    Note: The host operating system may have different\par
\tab\tab\tab    conventions regarding the end of file state from the\par
\tab\tab\tab    operating system that created the file; this is why end of\par
\tab\tab\tab    file detection is also carried out elsewhere in the program.\par
\tab\tab\tab    ASCII text files created for/by OS/8 or the P?S/8 SHELL\par
\tab\tab\tab    generally conform to this convention.  Extraneous characters\par
\tab\tab\tab    may exist in the file past the Control-Z sentinel character\par
                     which are to be ignored.  In extremely rare situations,\par
                     defective OS/8 utilities may create files that end on the last\par
                     character of the last file record with a typical character; as\par
                     such, a Control-Z sentinel character might not be present.\par
                     This situation is handled properly by the STRIP.TEC program.\par
                     However, it may be necessary to remedy this situation within\par
                     OS/8 to add a proper Control-Z sentinel character.  MS-DOS and\par
                     Windows do not support this convention internally; exact byte\par
                     counts are used to account for the proper file length.  P?S/8\par
                     conversion programs to/from OS/8 use safeguards against this\par
                     problem; OS/8 PIP operating in ASCII text mode should be used\par
                     to correct this problem as required.  Kermit-12 for OS/8\par
                     implements internal defensive measures to prevent this problem.\par
\par
."EONOCHARS$'\tab    If the edit buffer pointer is zero, there were no characters\par
\tab\tab\tab    in the buffer when the Control-Z sentinel character was\par
                     detected.  This is not a likely situation; however, this is\par
                     a portion of the "belts and suspenders" approach taken within\par
                     this program.  Should this condition apply, transfer control to\par
                     the portion of the program labeled \i !NOCHARS!\i0  bypassing all\par
                     commands beyond this point in the program.\page Line #4:\tab\tab    -3A-12"NONOFF$'.-3UZQZJ!NOFF!0,.P0,.K!NOCHARS!EFEX$\par
\par
-3A-12"NONOFF$'      The implementation of the OS/8 high-speed reader/punch device\par
                     handlers is quite poor.  The reader handler will generally drop\par
                     the last character which is problematic when the contents of\par
                     the data stream are not surrounded by leader/trailer codes.\par
\par
                     Note: It is not clear if the design logic actually applies to\par
                     properly adjusted hardware; it may have merely been a defensive\par
                     measure applied by an ignorant programmer not aware of the\par
                     maintenance issues of the PC04/PC05 reader/punch devices.  In\par
                     the PEPS environment, the notion of a malformed character does\par
                     not apply; as such, it is necessary to obtain every character.\par
                     Unfortunately, the design is such that a double-buffered\par
                     approach is taken that will predictably ignore the final\par
                     character.\line\line                      To remedy this problem, OS/8 PIP may be used to restore the\par
                     final character (generally a <LF>) or be certain the OS/8\par
                     program that will process the file ignores <LF> characters.\par
                     (OS/8 PAL8 delimits source lines with <CR> and ignores <LF>.)\par
\par
                     When using P?S/8 OS8CON to convert OS/8 input to P?S/8 output\par
                     files, this case is properly handled; when converting P?S/8\par
                     text files to OS/8 text files, the result is always properly\par
                     and completely formed with <CR> and <LF> on every line and a\par
                     Control-Z character is always provided as a sentinel character\par
                     to signify proper end-of-file.\par
\par
                     The punch handling is far worse:  A mis-feature intended only\par
                     for the Typeset-8 group was foisted on the general OS/8 user\par
                     community despite it having no constructive purpose.\par
\par
                     The mis-feature is the deliberate insertion of otherwise\par
                     extraneous characters to attempt to format long paper tapes to\par
                     be placed  into the reader stations of offline Teletype Model\par
                     ASR-35 terminals, which were generally available in all in-\par
                     house Typeset-8 installations.  A more sensible arrangement\par
                     would include a Typeset-8 or other program to properly handle\par
                     the particulars of this terminal; instead, the paper tape is\par
                     greatly lengthened because of badly implemented attempts to\par
                     directly print the output.\par
\par
                     While this conceptual approach is quite bad, the implementation\par
                     is even worse: Proper handling of the <HT> character on this\par
                     device depends on exact carriage position.  Depending on the\par
                     particulars, one or perhaps two fill characters must be placed\par
                     into the data stream directly to be self-contained.  A simple\par
                     formula \b should\b0  have been used to determine the proper fill\par
                     character count; instead a single <NUL> (or perhaps <RO>)\par
                     character is always used.  While this method does work in\par
                     nearly half of the applicable cases that apply, this causes the\par
                     output to be needlessly slowed down where the fill character is\par
                     not required, and when two fill characters are needed, it is\par
                     inadequate leading to character smear in the printing carriage.\par
\par
                     Various filler character strategies (both adequate and\par
                     inadequate) are used for the supported control characters,\par
                     including <FF>, <VT> and <HT>.  Quite pointlessly, different\par
                     fill characters are used in some cases different from others.\par
                     The approach used is sometimes simple-minded; the worst-case\par
                     number of fill characters is used instead of using a program\par
                     that can keep track of where on the printed page the current\par
                     carriage position is located.\page                      This utility is designed to eliminate every instance of\par
                     undesirable output to produce a properly formatted output\par
                     suitable for further processing.  As such, there are some\par
                     quirky cases that must be addressed in this section of the\par
                     program.\par
\par
                     While some of the associated overhead has already been removed\par
                     elsewhere, a specific case has been observed where a <FF>\par
                     followed by <CR>/<LF> is in the buffer just before the\par
                     Control-Z sentinel character.  Since one of the goals of the\par
                     program is to remove trailing <FF> characters, this section was\par
                     added to explicitly remove this undesirable character sequence.\par
\par
                     If the last three characters just before the Control-Z sentinel\par
                     character are <FF> <CR> <LF> they will be removed.  Checking\par
                     three characters before the Control-Z sentinel character,\par
                     compare that character to <FF>.  If not a match, proceed to the\par
                     next section of the program (labeled \i !NOFF!\i0 ).\par
\par
